# Line endings.
#
# The index is LF for essentially every text file already; this makes that a
# rule rather than a side effect of each contributor's core.autocrlf. Without
# it, one file committed from an autocrlf=false Windows checkout lands with
# CRLF in the index and every later edit shows as a whole-file rewrite —
# scripts/build_normalized_bundles.mts did exactly that (~880 lines of
# line-ending-only churn on a 12-line change, and git blame lost with it).
#
# text=auto: git decides what is text and stores it LF-normalized. Working
# trees still get CRLF on Windows via core.autocrlf — this governs the index,
# not the checkout. Binary files are detected and left alone; the LFS entries
# below carry an explicit -text for the same reason.
* text=auto

# Source and config: never guessed, always LF in the index.
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.mts   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.jsx   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
data/expression/neuroseq/source/catalog.json -text !eol whitespace=blank-at-eol,blank-at-eof,space-before-tab,cr-at-eol
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.R     text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
# Vitest writes snapshots with LF. A CRLF checkout made every test run rewrite
# the file and leave it modified.
*.snap  text eol=lf

# Tabular data files are text; keep them LF so a re-export does not rewrite
# every row. .htaccess ships to an Apache/LiteSpeed host that wants LF.
*.tsv       text eol=lf
*.csv       text eol=lf

# Generated dataset bundles are pinned by exact-byte SHA-256 in the expression
# and retained evidence ledgers. Checkout must not renormalize their bytes or
# a local rebuild can replace a valid pin with a platform-specific checksum.
public/datasets/**/*.js -text !eol

